> # System design: Arachne Alpha Factory
> 
> ## 1. Prediction target
> 
> Your target is:
> 
> \[
> \Delta p_{i,t}^{(n)} = p_{i,t+n} - p_{i,t}
> \]
> 
> for ticker \(i\), horizon \(n = \texttt{nday}\).
> 
> But for modeling, I would not directly predict raw price change alone. That is scale-dependent and awkward across tickers. Instead I would:
> 
> - Train on **future log-return**:
> \[
> y_{i,t}^{(n)} = \log p_{i,t+n} - \log p_{i,t}
> \]
> 
> Then convert back to your required output:
> 
> \[
> \widehat{\Delta p}_{i,t}^{(n)} = p_{i,t}\left(\exp(\hat y_{i,t}^{(n)}) - 1\right)
> \]
> 
> That makes cross-stock learning much more coherent.
> 
> ---
> 
> ## 2. Feature layer: price-based signals that are actually useful
> 
> Even if all we have right now is high/low history, we can do much better than drift.
> 
> From midpoint price \(m_{i,t} = (h_{i,t}+l_{i,t})/2\), and range \(q_{i,t} = h_{i,t}-l_{i,t}\), build features like:
> 
> ### Trend / momentum
> - 1d, 5d, 20d, 60d, 120d returns
> - Exponentially weighted momentum
> - Moving average gaps:
> \[
> \log m_{i,t} - \text{MA}_{k}(\log m_{i,\cdot})
> \]
> - Breakout indicators:
>   - distance to rolling max/min
> - Trend acceleration:
>   - short momentum minus long momentum
> 
> ### Mean reversion
> - Deviation from rolling mean in z-score units
> - Recent reversal:
>   - 1d return after 20d run-up
> - Bollinger-style standardized gap
> 
> ### Volatility / risk
> - Rolling std of returns over multiple windows
> - Parkinson-style range volatility from highs/lows:
> \[
> \sigma^2 \propto (\log(h_{i,t}/l_{i,t}))^2
> \]
> - Volatility of volatility
> - Range expansion/compression
> 
> ### Microstructure-ish proxies from daily bars
> - Relative intraday range:
> \[
> (h_{i,t} - l_{i,t}) / m_{i,t}
> \]
> - Range trend
> - Gap proxies if you later get open/close
> 
> ### Relative strength
> - Cross-sectional rank of momentum
> - Cross-sectional rank of volatility-adjusted momentum
> - Return relative to equal-weight market return
> - Beta-ish exposure to market proxy
> 
> ### Regime features
> - Market trend over last 20/60 days
> - Market volatility
> - Cross-sectional dispersion
> - Fraction of stocks above moving average
> - Correlation regime proxy
> 
> These are cheap and surprisingly strong when combined well.
> 
> ---
> 
> ## 3. Base models
> 
> I would not trust any single model family. I’d make several compete.
> 
> ## A. Linear shrinkage model
> A regularized linear model:
> - ridge
> - lasso
> - elastic net
> 
> This captures broad, stable effects and gives interpretability.
> 
> Model:
> \[
> y_{i,t}^{(n)} = x_{i,t}'\beta + \epsilon_{i,t}
> \]
> 
> Good baseline, low variance.
> 
> ---
> 
> ## B. Tree-based nonlinear model
> For example:
> - gradient boosted trees
> - random forest
> 
> These capture thresholds and interactions:
> - momentum works unless volatility is too high
> - mean reversion is stronger in high-dispersion markets
> - breakouts differ by regime
> 
> Julia options exist, though if package friction becomes annoying, we can keep this part lightweight or bridge to Python later.
> 
> ---
> 
> ## C. Cross-sectional rank model
> Sometimes it’s easier to predict **which stocks will outperform**, not exact returns.
> 
> Train a model to predict:
> \[
> \Pr(y_{i,t}^{(n)} \text{ in top decile at } t)
> \]
> 
> or a normalized cross-sectional return.
> 
> This is often more robust in stock selection settings.
> 
> ---
> 
> ## D. Time-series specialist per stock
> For each stock, fit a tiny local model:
> - recent momentum
> - recent mean reversion
> - volatility scaling
> 
> These weak specialists sometimes help for idiosyncratic names.
> 
> ---
> 
> ## E. Market-regime specialist
> Fit separate models for:
> - high-vol regime
> - low-vol regime
> - bullish market breadth
> - bearish market breadth
> 
> Then route prediction through the relevant specialist.
> 
> ---
> 
> # 4. Meta-model / ensemble
> 
> This is the important part.
> 
> Each base model emits a prediction:
> \[
> \hat y_{i,t}^{(n,m)}
> \]
> for model \(m\).
> 
> Then combine them:
> \[
> \hat y_{i,t}^{(n)} = \sum_m w_{m,t}^{(n)} \hat y_{i,t}^{(n,m)}
> \]
> 
> Where weights can be:
> 
> - fixed equal weights initially
> - then performance-based
> - eventually regime-dependent
> 
> For example:
> - if trend regime is strong, weight momentum/tree model more
> - if market is choppy, weight mean-reversion and shrinkage more
> 
> A very practical meta-weighting rule is:
> - rolling inverse error weighting
> - exponentially decayed recent validation performance
> - capped so one model doesn’t dominate too hard
> 
> This is how you get adaptivity without overengineering.
> 
> ---
> 
> # 5. Training structure
> 
> Because you’ll presumably rerun this regularly, use **walk-forward validation**.
> 
> For each horizon \(n\):
> 1. Build training examples \((x_{i,t}, y_{i,t}^{(n)})\)
> 2. Split time into rolling train/validation windows
> 3. Fit base models on past
> 4. Score on next window
> 5. Learn ensemble weights from those scores
> 6. Refit on all available past
> 7. Predict current \(t \to t+n\)
> 
> This avoids leakage and gives the meta-model something honest to learn from.
> 
> ---
